                        "glosses": [
                            "A thumping sound."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "informal"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "cba72a88-d912-39ce-be22-e0d687781263",
                        "entry_id": "093271d2-74f6-3957-9089-91e3edfb4cd2",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A genre of fan fiction in which a character endures injury, torture, or other forms of physical and mental suffering."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "slang"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "fc9ed1dc-b1d0-4e98-86ec-0dcfea87c9c7",
                                "sense_id": "cba72a88-d912-39ce-be22-e0d687781263",
                                "text": "By embracing dark genres of slash such as ‘squick’ and ‘whump’, the art of mythagowood purposely skirts parameters of taste and acceptability."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9c2f7ee1-b5e3-4d5a-b6d4-7a4a3f43e92e",
                                "sense_id": "cba72a88-d912-39ce-be22-e0d687781263",
                                "text": "Narrative devices shared by Hannibal and fanfiction include “whump,” the vivid abuse and torture of a character, a popular convention across certain types of fanfiction: Graham is knocked out, shot, stabbed, gutted, sawed open, and subjected to induced seizures."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "99bce035-3dac-45ea-aae0-8c15090879c0",
                                "sense_id": "cba72a88-d912-39ce-be22-e0d687781263",
                                "text": "However, further research with a larger sample and encompassing more fandoms would be necessary to confirm this potential explanation of this trend and to explore the impact of other factors, such as assigning characters a physical and/or mental disability or illness for the purpose of whump or hurt/comfort stories."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
